WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.350
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book four.

2
00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:09.559
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.859 --> 00:00:15.634
Unit eight: Risk Management. Today you will assess decisions by weighing X against Y.

4
00:00:15.934 --> 00:00:18.285
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.233 --> 00:00:22.733
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.583 --> 00:00:24.991
Risk assessment.

7
00:00:25.983 --> 00:00:30.073
Risk assessment means a systematic evaluation of possible dangers.

8
00:00:30.683 --> 00:00:34.053
A risk assessment was completed before the new warehouse opened.

9
00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:37.581
Your turn. Say: Risk assessment.

10
00:00:40.433 --> 00:00:41.980
Risk assessment.

11
00:00:42.717 --> 00:00:43.834
Mitigation.

12
00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:48.637
Mitigation means action taken to reduce the severity of a risk.

13
00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:52.678
Flood barriers are a mitigation measure for riverside shops.

14
00:00:53.283 --> 00:00:55.970
Your turn. Say: Mitigation.

15
00:00:58.833 --> 00:00:59.788
Mitigation.

16
00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:01.748
Contingency.

17
00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:06.363
Contingency means a plan prepared for a possible future event.

18
00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:10.064
Our contingency plan covers a two-week power cut.

19
00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:13.384
Your turn. Say: Contingency.

20
00:01:16.233 --> 00:01:17.478
Contingency.

21
00:01:18.217 --> 00:01:19.334
Exposure.

22
00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:24.137
Exposure means the degree to which a firm could be affected by a risk.

23
00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:28.387
Borrowing in dollars increases exposure to currency changes.

24
00:01:28.983 --> 00:01:32.193
Your turn. Say: Exposure.

25
00:01:35.033 --> 00:01:36.209
Exposure.

26
00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:38.160
Probability.

27
00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:41.997
Probability means how likely something is to happen.

28
00:01:42.583 --> 00:01:45.314
The probability of a major flood this year is low.

29
00:01:45.917 --> 00:01:48.999
Your turn. Say: Probability.

30
00:01:51.833 --> 00:01:52.985
Probability.

31
00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:54.670
Impact.

32
00:01:55.683 --> 00:01:58.496
Impact means the effect or consequence of an event.

33
00:01:59.083 --> 00:02:02.813
A cyber-attack would have a severe impact on customer trust.

34
00:02:03.417 --> 00:02:06.197
Your turn. Say: Impact.

35
00:02:09.033 --> 00:02:10.000
Impact.

36
00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:11.960
Insurance.

37
00:02:12.950 --> 00:02:17.399
Insurance means an arrangement that pays compensation for specified losses.

38
00:02:17.983 --> 00:02:20.494
Insurance covered most of the fire damage.

39
00:02:21.083 --> 00:02:23.492
Your turn. Say: Insurance.

40
00:02:26.333 --> 00:02:27.636
Insurance.

41
00:02:28.383 --> 00:02:29.501
Resilience.

42
00:02:30.517 --> 00:02:34.374
Resilience means the capacity to absorb shocks and keep operating.

43
00:02:34.983 --> 00:02:38.167
Backup generators strengthened the factory's resilience.

44
00:02:38.783 --> 00:02:41.865
Your turn. Say: Resilience.

45
00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:45.898
Resilience.

46
00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:50.583
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:51.417 --> 00:02:57.703
Visal, the tourism company wants to borrow heavily to build a hotel in Kep. Is that too risky?

48
00:02:58.817 --> 00:03:07.959
We need to weigh the potential profit against the exposure. If tourism falls, as it did in 2020, the debt still has to be repaid.

49
00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:12.025
But the probability of another pandemic is low.

50
00:03:13.117 --> 00:03:21.818
Perhaps, yet the impact would be catastrophic. Good decisions weigh probability against impact rather than focusing on one alone.

51
00:03:22.917 --> 00:03:24.684
So should they abandon the project?

52
00:03:25.783 --> 00:03:34.728
Not necessarily. They could build in phases and buy insurance, which balances the upside against a manageable level of risk.

53
00:03:35.867 --> 00:03:37.216
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:41.094
Grammar focus: Weighing X against Y.

55
00:03:41.394 --> 00:03:42.535
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:43.250 --> 00:03:46.213
Good decisions weigh probability against impact.

57
00:03:47.117 --> 00:03:50.126
The board weighed the potential profit against the debt.

58
00:03:51.017 --> 00:03:54.932
Investors must set expected returns against possible losses.

59
00:03:55.867 --> 00:04:04.777
'Weigh X against Y' means compare two factors to reach a decision. The noun form is also common: 'After weighing the costs against the benefits, we decided…'

60
00:04:05.517 --> 00:04:09.249
Be careful! Don't say: We weighed the risk with the reward.

61
00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:13.966
Say: We weighed the risk against the reward.

62
00:04:14.266 --> 00:04:15.836
Weigh X against Y.

63
00:04:16.633 --> 00:04:19.193
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:21.802
Thinking in Probabilities.

65
00:04:22.102 --> 00:04:28.839
Risk management begins with identifying what could go wrong and estimating both its probability and its potential impact.

66
00:04:29.833 --> 00:04:35.815
A rare event with catastrophic consequences may deserve more attention than a frequent but minor inconvenience.

67
00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:44.465
Managers then choose whether to avoid, reduce, transfer or accept each risk, weighing the cost of mitigation against the expected loss.

68
00:04:45.467 --> 00:04:52.319
Insurance transfers some risk to others, while contingency plans ensure the business can keep operating when disaster strikes.

69
00:04:53.333 --> 00:05:00.267
Crucially, risk management is not about eliminating risk, since no profit is possible without taking some chances.

70
00:05:01.367 --> 00:05:05.183
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:06.017 --> 00:05:08.399
We must weigh the benefits blank the costs.

72
00:05:08.699 --> 00:05:12.086
A, against. B, with. C, between.

73
00:05:15.633 --> 00:05:17.505
The answer is A: against.

74
00:05:18.650 --> 00:05:21.938
Action taken to reduce the severity of a risk is blank.

75
00:05:22.238 --> 00:05:26.321
A, exposure. B, mitigation. C, probability.

76
00:05:29.867 --> 00:05:31.866
The answer is B: mitigation.

77
00:05:33.017 --> 00:05:36.224
A plan prepared for a possible future event is a blank.

78
00:05:36.524 --> 00:05:40.746
A, contingency. B, risk assessment. C, resilience.

79
00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:46.288
The answer is A: contingency.

80
00:05:47.450 --> 00:05:49.287
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:49.587 --> 00:05:58.686
A, After weighing the options, we chose the safer one.. B, After weigh the options, we chose the safer one.. C, After weighing against the options, we chose safer..

82
00:06:02.233 --> 00:06:05.800
The answer is A: After weighing the options, we chose the safer one..

83
00:06:06.950 --> 00:06:09.960
According to the reading, risk management is NOT about…

84
00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:16.340
A, estimating impact. B, eliminating all risk. C, making contingency plans.

85
00:06:19.900 --> 00:06:22.318
The answer is B: eliminating all risk.

86
00:06:23.533 --> 00:06:26.580
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:27.417 --> 00:06:30.066
How would you assess whether a risk is worth taking?

88
00:06:46.850 --> 00:06:48.304
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:48.604 --> 00:07:25.096
To assess whether a risk is worth taking, I would first weigh the possible reward against the worst realistic outcome. Next, I would estimate the probability of failure and ask whether the business could survive it. For instance, opening a second shop with savings might be acceptable, whereas borrowing against the family home could be disastrous if sales disappoint. Finally, I would look for ways to reduce the risk, such as starting small or buying insurance. A risk is worth taking when the downside is survivable.

90
00:07:25.396 --> 00:07:27.325
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:28.217 --> 00:07:32.634
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and weighing x against y.

92
00:07:32.934 --> 00:07:37.432
Today's value is prudence. Think carefully about risks before you act.

93
00:07:37.732 --> 00:07:40.384
See you in Unit nine. Goodbye!

94
00:07:40.684 --> 00:07:43.464
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

